Removing old varnish is a delicate job. The conservator tests liquids on a tiny corner, about the size of a fingernail. The right one softens the varnish but leaves the paint alone.

Then comes cleaning, swab by swab. A large painting can take months or even years. As the brown layer comes off, colours that nobody alive had seen suddenly appear: bright blue skies, pink cheeks, gold threads.

{{story:art-forgery}}

Mozart was born in Salzburg, in today's Austria. His father, Leopold, was a musician and teacher. Wolfgang and his older sister, Nannerl, were both brilliant at the keyboard. Wolfgang was writing short pieces by the age of five.

Leopold took his children on long tours to show them off at royal courts. Between 1763 and 1766, the family travelled through Germany, [[@paris|Paris]] and [[@london|London]], where Wolfgang met the composer Johann Christian Bach. In London, aged about eight, he wrote his first symphony. Travel in coaches over bumpy roads was slow and tiring, and the children were often ill.

{{story:london}}

Beats usually come in groups. In much Western music, they are [[@maths-music-rhythm|counted in fours]], "ONE two three four", with the first beat a little stronger. A waltz counts in threes: "ONE two three". Each group is called a bar.

Indian classical music uses longer cycles called *tala*. Teentaal has 16 beats in four groups of four. Musicians keep track by clapping on some beats and waving on others. The first beat, *sam*, is the home base. A drummer may play a long, twisting pattern that seems to get lost, then lands exactly on sam, and the audience cheers.

