Keibul Lamjao was set up to protect the sangai, a brow-antlered deer found nowhere else in the wild. Its [[antler|antlers]] sweep forward over its eyes in a long curve. In 1951, it was even declared [[extinct|extinct]], until a small group was found again on the phumdis in 1953.

Sangai pick their way across the floating ground with high, careful steps. Manipuris call it the dancing deer, and it appears in songs and in the graceful movements of [[@manipuri-dance|Manipuri dance]]. It's Manipur's state animal. A count in 2016 found about 260, so it remains [[endangered|endangered]].

{{story:manipuri-dance}}

When moist air rises, it cools. Cool air can't hold as much water vapour as warm air, so the vapour condenses into droplets and forms clouds. When the droplets grow heavy enough, they fall. You can follow this in [[@clouds-rain|clouds and rain]].

The Khasi Hills rise sharply from the plains to about 1,400 m in only a short distance. The [[monsoon|monsoon]] winds have nowhere to go but up, and the air is wrung out like a wet towel. Rain can fall for days without stopping. Some valleys and slopes funnel the clouds even more, which is why a few villages get far more rain than others just a short drive away.

